<h1>How it was built</h1>
<p class="sub">
    Reconcile is a demonstration of AI-assisted development with review
    discipline. Claude Code proposed the project, designed it, and wrote the
    code and the tests that check it. Dustin Mennie picked the target, set the
    rules below and decided what shipped. No C# had been written by the author
    before this project.
</p>

<h2>A conventions file, and something that checks each rule</h2>
<p class="sub">
    Every AI session starts by reading <a href="@Repo/blob/main/CLAUDE.md">CLAUDE.md</a>:
    what the project is, how it is laid out, and the rules its code is held to.
    A rule only counts if something checks it, so each one below names what does.
</p>
<div class="tablewrap">
    <table class="rules">
        <thead><tr><th>Rule in CLAUDE.md</th><th>What holds it</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Nothing is dropped silently</td>
                <td><code>EveryInputAppearsInExactlyOneResult</code>, <code>Import_CollectsBadRows_InsteadOfDroppingThemSilently</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Ambiguous matches are left unmatched, never guessed</td>
                <td><code>AmountDate_TwoCandidates_ClaimsNeither</code>, <code>AmbiguousGift_NamesBothSettlements_AndEachSettlementNamesTheGift</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Findings are records, not counts</td>
                <td><code>EveryFinding_SaysWhy_AndCleanMatchesStayQuiet</code>; every tile on a run filters to the rows behind it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The report ties out</td>
                <td><code>Bridge_TiesSettledGrossToCrmTotal_ToTheCent</code>, <code>Bridge_StopsTyingOut_WhenAResultGoesMissing</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Strong rules claim before weak ones</td>
                <td><code>StrongRuleRunsBeforeWeakRule</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The demo's own claims are true</td>
                <td><code>AnswerKey_AgreesWithTheGeneratorAndTheEngine</code>, <code>EachKindOfEdit_LandsWhereItIsPredicted</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Money is <code>decimal(12,2)</code>, never float</td>
                <td>Declared on every money column in <code>ReconcileDbContext</code>; read in each migration before it is applied</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>One migration per schema change; never edit an applied one</td>
                <td>Four migrations, each additive: <code>InitialSchema</code>, <code>AddImportNotes</code>, <code>AddResultNote</code>, <code>AddRunScenario</code></td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2>Two bugs the checks caught</h2>
<p class="sub">
    A check is only worth something if it can fail. These two did, before
    anyone used the result.
</p>
<div class="caught-list">
    <div>
        <b>Try to break it found one on its first run.</b>
        The amount-and-date rule is meant only for gifts with no reference, but
        it was also pairing gifts that carried a mistyped one: a duplicated $25
        settlement was matched to an unrelated $25 gift. The rule now does what
        its documentation says, and a test holds it there.
    </div>
    <div>
        <b>The report's tie-out could not fail.</b>
        Records were loaded only through their results, so a record the engine
        dropped would never have been loaded, and the bridge would still have
        shown $0.00. Records now load in their own right, and a test removes
        results and expects the bridge to stop tying.
    </div>
</div>

